{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ba8fe9e6-2b5c-4724-972a-b0090d7809aa","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Start with sine as a dashed reference. Its amplitude is one, and one full cycle takes two pi.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Compare two sine graphs"},{"op":"figure","id":"waves","region":"right","frame":{"x":[0,6.6],"y":[-2.5,2.5],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x (radians)","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"base","y":"sin(x)","domain":[0,6.283185307],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[4.6,1.8],"text":"Dashed: sin x","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"baseline","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"y=\\sin x:\\quad A=1,\\quad T=2\\pi"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ba8fe9e6-2b5c-4724-972a-b0090d7809aa","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Multiplying outside sine doubles every height. The peaks reach two, but the period stays two pi.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"stretch","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"doubleheight","y":"2*sin(x)","domain":[0,6.283185307]},{"kind":"point","id":"highpeak","at":{"on":"doubleheight","x":1.570796327},"label":"2"},{"kind":"segment","from":[1.570796327,0],"to":{"at":"highpeak"},"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[2.8,2.15],"text":"2 sin x","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"heightfact","anchor":{"el":"baseline","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"y=2\\sin x:\\quad A=2,\\quad T=2\\pi"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ba8fe9e6-2b5c-4724-972a-b0090d7809aa","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Multiplying inside sine makes the phase advance twice as fast. Sine of two x completes two cycles where ordinary sine completes one.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"compress","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"doublefreq","y":"sin(2*x)","domain":[0,6.283185307]},{"kind":"point","id":"smallpeak","at":{"on":"doublefreq","x":0.785398163},"label":"1"},{"kind":"point","id":"nextpeak","at":{"on":"doublefreq","x":3.926990817}},{"kind":"measure","from":{"at":"smallpeak"},"to":{"at":"nextpeak"},"label":"π"},{"kind":"text","at":[5.6,1.35],"text":"sin 2x","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"periodfact","anchor":{"el":"heightfact","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"y=\\sin(2x):\\quad A=1,\\quad T=\\frac{2\\pi}{2}=\\pi"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"ba8fe9e6-2b5c-4724-972a-b0090d7809aa","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s4"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ba8fe9e6-2b5c-4724-972a-b0090d7809aa","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Outside changes height; inside changes period. At pi over two, which graph reaches two, and which graph crosses zero?","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"comparepoints","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"freqzero","at":{"on":"doublefreq","x":1.570796327},"label":"0"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.570796327,-2.25],"text":"x = π/2","size":"small"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"general","anchor":{"el":"periodfact","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"y=a\\sin(bx):\\quad A=|a|,\\quad T=\\frac{2\\pi}{|b|}\\quad(b\\neq0)"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"At x = π/2, what are the values of 2 sin x and sin 2x?","expected_gist":"2 sin(π/2) = 2, while sin(2 · π/2) = sin π = 0."}}}
